<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1582 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gaudete</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A joyful Latin Christmas song printed in 1582. In 1973 a British folk band made it a pop hit, singing it with no instruments.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (the standard tune comes from older liturgical books)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Finnish and Swedish Christians, for whom Piae Cantiones was printed; earlier the Hussites of the Jistebnice hymnal</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Greifswald, Germany (first printed)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>thought to be 16th century; an entry from around 1420 in the Jistebnice hymnal; printed 1582</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous Latin words, published in Piae Cantiones (1582))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Gaudete&quot; (&quot;Rejoice&quot;) is a sacred Christmas carol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, thought to have been composed in the 16th century, though there is a known entry from around 1420 in the Hussite Jistebnice hymnal (Jistebnický kancionál). It was published in 1582 in Piae Cantiones, a collection of Finnish and Swedish sacred songs printed in the North German city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>. The collection gives no music for the verses; the standard tune comes from older liturgical books.</p><p>The text is a typical medieval song of praise and follows the usual pattern of its time: a uniform series of four-line stanzas, each preceded by a two-line refrain, which in the early English carol was called the burden. Carols of this kind could be on any subject, but most were about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, the saints or the Christmas season.</p><p>The carol became widely known in the 1970s. The British folk rock group Steeleye Span recorded it a cappella, and the single reached number 14 in the UK singles chart in 1973. Their guitarist Bob Johnson had heard it at a folk-carol service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, which he attended with his father-in-law. It is one of only two British top-20 hits sung entirely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, the other being Sarah Brightman and Paul Miles-Kingston's &quot;Pie Jesu&quot; of 1986, and one of only a handful of a cappella hits. When the band performed it on Top of the Pops, the resident dance troupe walked on in medieval-style robes holding candles.</p><p>Since then it has been recorded by choirs, early-music ensembles and rock bands alike: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Camerata under Joel Cohen (1985), the Finnish singer Pertti Neumann (1986), the King's Singers (1990), Anúna with Eimear Quinn (1996), the Mediæval Bæbes (1997), the boy choir Libera (2001), Tenebrae in an arrangement by Karl Jenkins (2004), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> (2004), St Paul's Cathedral Choir under Andrew Carwood (2015) and the synth-pop group Erasure, whose electronic version of 2013 was the first single from their album Snow Globe. It has also been parodied, and Steeleye Span's version is sung along to in a car in the television comedy I'm Alan Partridge.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Christmas call to rejoice. A short refrain urging the listener to rejoice comes round before every four-line stanza of praise. The mood is festive and lively.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gaudēte, gaudēte!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rejoice, rejoice!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Chrīstus est nātus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ is born</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ex Marīā virgine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the Virgin Mary –</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">gaudēte!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rejoice!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tempus adest grātiae</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The time of grace has come—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Hoc quod optābāmus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What we have wished for;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Carmina laetitiae</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Songs of joy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dēvōtē reddāmus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us give back faithfully.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Deus homō factus est</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God has become man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nātūrā mirante,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With nature marvelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mundus renovātus est</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The world has been renewed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ā Chrīsto regnante.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the reigning Christ.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ezechiēlis porta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The closed gate of Ezekiel</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Clausa pertrānsītur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is passed through,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Unde lūx est orta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whence the light is risen;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Salūs invenītur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Salvation has been found.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ergō nostra cōntiō</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Therefore, let our assembly</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Psallat iam in lūstrō;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now sing in brightness</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Benedīcat Dominō:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let it bless the Lord:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Salūs Regī nostrō.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Greetings to our King.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous; the Latin text of Piae Cantiones (1582), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaudete" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English translation from Wikipedia's article (CC BY-SA). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaudete" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
